\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Two lifts of each signed coordinate change}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{For every nonzero spin carrier and grading in the
\vocabk{lift of the signed coordinate group}{fdip-016P}, the
surjection of \ref{fdip-016R} has central kernel
#{\{\bbI_S,-\bbI_S\}}. The order of
#{\operatorname{Pin}(B_\Lambda;\Gamma)} is #{2|B_\Lambda|}.
Its subgroup over the determinant-one coordinate changes is
generated by #{-\bbI_S}, the lifted equal-period quarter-turns
and the paired flips #{f_j f_k} for axes of distinct periods.}

\proof{
\p{In the algebra of \ref{fdip-016T} take the group generated by
the unit vectors #{d_j} and the elements
##{\frac{1-d_jd_k}{\sqrt{2}}
      = d_j\frac{-d_j-d_k}{\sqrt{2}}
\qquad(j\ne k,\ N_j=N_k).}
Both factors are unit vectors, as are their inverses
#{w^{-1}=-w}. Under signed conjugation each
#{d_j} flips its own axis. Writing #{w=(-d_j-d_k)/\sqrt{2}},
reflection perpendicular to #{w} sends #{d_j} to #{-d_k} and
#{d_k} to #{-d_j}. The subsequent reflection perpendicular to
#{d_j} takes the latter to #{d_j}. These are the generators of \ref{fdip-016R}.}

\p{A word in the kernel has even parity because its orthogonal
image has determinant one. It therefore commutes with each
#{d_j} by the signed conjugation formula. Conjugation by #{d_j}
acts diagonally on the ordered monomial basis. For a subset of
size #{t} the sign is #{(-1)^t} if #{j} is absent and
#{(-1)^{t-1}} if #{j} occurs. Any nonempty even subset contains
a generator giving sign minus one. Independence of the ordered
products leaves only a real scalar. Reversing products and
sending #{d_j} to #{-d_j} respects the defining relations.
This reversing operation takes every unit vector to its inverse,
hence takes each group word to its inverse. It fixes real
scalars, so a scalar word is #{1} or #{-1}. Both occur since
#{d_1^2=-1}.}

\p{The assignment #{d_j\mapsto f_j} defines a unital real
algebra map by the relations in \ref{fdip-016R}. It sends the
two-vector generator to #{u_{jk}} since
#{f_j f_k=-\varepsilon_j\varepsilon_k}, so it maps the word group
onto the represented lift. Ordinary conjugation on the represented
directions induces the very same signed permutation on every
generator. The directions are independent by \ref{fdip-016R}.
Consequently a word acting identically on #{S} has trivial
orthogonal image and belongs to #{\{1,-1\}}. Since #{S\ne0}
over #{\mathbb C}, its two scalar images differ. Thus the group
map on the words is injective. Each fiber over #{B_\Lambda}
is a coset of its two-element scalar kernel, giving the order.}

\p{Given a determinant-one signed permutation, first match its
underlying permutation with quarter-turns, one for each needed
transposition in an equal-period block. What remains flips an
even number of signs. Pair the reversed axes. Within a block
a quarter-turn squared flips any chosen pair. Across two blocks
the image of #{f_j f_k} does so. This gives all determinant-one
images. A lift of such an image differs from a product of the
listed lifts by a kernel sign, itself included. Every listed lift
has determinant-one image. In a single dimension the kernel sign
alone generates this subgroup.}
}
